{"qid":"Q943191","names":["Lakhmid Kingdom"],"title":"Lakhmid Kingdom","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Lakhmid Kingdom was a pre-Islamic Arab client state headquartered at al-Hira (near modern Kufa, Iraq), which served as a buffer and proxy for the Sasanian Empire against both Byzantine-allied Arab tribes and nomadic incursions across Mesopotamia's western desert fringe. The Lakhmids reached their greatest prestige under al-Mundhir III (r. ca. 503–554), who inflicted a decisive defeat on the Byzantines and their Ghassanid clients at the Battle of Callinicum in 531 and built al-Hira into a noted center of Arabic poetry. The Sasanian shah Khosrow II dissolved the client kingdom in 602, executing the last Lakhmid king al-Nu'man III and replacing dynastic rule with direct Persian governors — a decision that left the Arabian frontier undefended and may have facilitated the rapid Arab-Muslim conquest of 633–637.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The Lakhmid political core at al-Hira is well documented through Sasanian court records, Syriac chronicles, and Arabic literary tradition; the tribal hinterland they nominally controlled across the Syrian and Arabian deserts is loosely attested and reconstructed mainly from tribal genealogies.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Lakhmid dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Lakhmid-dynasty"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Lakhmid kingdom","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lakhmid_kingdom"}],"britannica":"topic/Lakhmid-dynasty","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Lakhmid kingdom","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Amr ibn Adi","nativeName":"عمرو بن عدي","roman":"ʿAmr i